Hi [~accountid:5e2af09cc55f580c9fca70a7]  and [~accountid:61556c1b7a6be40071b18e22] 

I have reviewed Alistair’s reply. Below you may see my edits to the contract with my comments ({color:#ff5630}in red{color}) as regards to his reply:

# I deleted his right to add a daily interest of 1.5% to accounts that fall to more than 90 days in arrears. - {color:#ff5630}General approach is to exclude any interest to be charged from Bolt, however, if it acceptable from you side it can still be in the contract.{color}
# I excluded the *indemnification* by Bolt of all liability, including all actions, claims, damages, costs and legal fees, which Alistair may incur arising out of actions taken or statements made by Alistair at Bolt’s direction or based upon information provided by Bolt, and any and all losses, claims, damages, expenses or liabilities related to the use of Bolt products or services. - {color:#ff5630}This clause is my main concern. Under general rule it is usually excluded by legal team as not acceptable by Bolt, as it potentially may entail a material risk to Bolt. In simple words Bolt agrees to pay for potential losses or damages incurred or caused by Alistair. However, it should be a business decision either to accept this risk or not.{color}
# I added the general clause for Alistair’s liability under New South Wales local law. - {color:#ff5630}It is weird that the service provider insists on excluding this clause. I cannot advice on New South Wales law, however by generally even if there is no such clause in agreement and no limitation of liability is expressly provided in the contract still general liability rules under local regulation should be applicable.{color}

To summarise the above I see the main risk in the indemnity clause and I cannot recommend to accept it. Hence, it is up to your decision either to accept it or not.

If you accept Alistair’s reply, I will prepare the final version for your approval.

